# Build/test environment only. Gradle does not load this file automatically.
# Export these in your shell (or configure the SDK/JDK in Android Studio).
# Do not put SIP passwords, signing passwords or provider credentials here.

# Absolute path to JDK 17. On macOS: /usr/libexec/java_home -v 17
JAVA_HOME=

# Android SDK containing platform android-36 and its build tools.
ANDROID_HOME=

# Installed NDK r28 or later. Used by scripts/build-android-pjsip.sh.
ANDROID_NDK_ROOT=

# Optional native build parallelism; defaults to 8.
JOBS=8

# Optional release signing. Export ALL FOUR for :app:assembleRelease or
# :app:bundleRelease; leave all blank for compile-only unsigned outputs.
# A partial configuration fails. Gradle verifies the keystore/alias/passwords.
# This file documents the names only; never populate and commit it.
# Use an absolute path to a private keystore outside the repository. Keep its
# backup and passwords in your password/secret manager, not source control.
# For Google Play App Signing, these identify your UPLOAD key. Play signs the
# distributed app with its app-signing key; use that certificate for Firebase
# package restrictions. Direct APK downloads retain the configured key, so
# plan their update/signing identity before distributing either channel.
# Debug builds always use the standard debug key, independently of these values.
SIPPER_ANDROID_SIGNING_STORE_FILE=
SIPPER_ANDROID_SIGNING_STORE_PASSWORD=
SIPPER_ANDROID_SIGNING_KEY_ALIAS=
SIPPER_ANDROID_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD=

# Optional adb serial for a disposable emulator when running native tests.
# Find it with adb devices. Do not use an unrelated physical phone.
ANDROID_SERIAL=

# Optional debug-build management origin. Export before running Gradle.
# Release builds always use https://sipper.dev and ignore this override.
# Use the exact same origin as the portal's SIPPER_BASE_URL. No path, credentials,
# query string or fragment is allowed. HTTPS is required except in debug builds
# for localhost, 127.0.0.1 or the Android emulator host alias 10.0.2.2.
# Example for a local portal reachable from the emulator: http://localhost:3100
SIPPER_MANAGED_URL=https://sipper.dev

# The client is public OAuth client sipper-android and has no client secret.
# Production redirect: com.hybes.sipper:/oauth/callback
# Debug redirect: com.hybes.sipper.development:/oauth/callback
# Register the debug redirect in the LOCAL portal's SIPPER_NATIVE_REDIRECTS,
# then run that portal's native-client seed step. Never add debug HTTP origins
# or development redirects to production merely to make local tests pass.

# The Directory tab uses this same origin/session; no directory key is needed.
# Server SIPPER_CONFIG_TTL_SECONDS bounds shared-contact cache validity.
# Cache: 10,000 contacts / 8 MB contact text per customer, with a 16 MB aggregate
# managed-session ceiling. Sign-out, expiry and known access withdrawal clear it.
# Native directory calling against the actual local portal passes the isolated
# test:android-portal harness. Ordinary-user browser handoff and populated messaging
# are also checked separately; physical directory calling remains unverified.

# Directory → Messages uses the same enrolled customer, OAuth session and origin.
# No messaging client key is required. Apply portal migrations 032 and 033 before upgrading
# clients. Server-only SIPPER_MESSAGE_RETENTION_DAYS is in portal/.env.example.
# Bodies, drafts and uncertain-send UUIDs remain in the foreground view only:
# no message archive, Android saved-state entry or additional vault is created.
# Pages expire independently within 30 seconds and poll every five seconds.
# Responses are limited to 2,000,000 bytes; pages contain at most 100 rows and text
# is limited to 4,096 UTF-16 code units. An explicit retry reuses the original ID
# and exact text. Membership/seat withdrawal, disconnect and sign-out deny access.
# The local Android portal harness verifies real direct/group messages, peer
# replies, shared read state, group changes/leaving/rejoining and encrypted server
# envelopes with fictional users. Ordinary-user interactive messaging, native
# group rename/save and draft controls are checked; other group controls,
# physical devices and production delivery remain unverified.
# Apply portal migration 034 for chat alerts. They use a separate encrypted server
# token registration with the same FCM client settings below, no new client key.
# Enable Message notifications under Accounts; it defaults off and only needs
# Android notification permission, independently of microphone/call permissions.
# Alerts use the Messages channel and generic "Sipper / New message" text only.
# Taps use an already owned connection and authenticated HTTP to open a conversation.
# Real FCM delivery and notification permission/tap UI remain unverified.
# Local portal acceptance refuses a QA emulator clock more than ten seconds from
# the host. Keep automatic time accurate; never relax response expiry to fix a
# paused emulator's clock drift.

# Optional Firebase Cloud Messaging client configuration. Leave ALL FOUR blank
# to build and use standalone SIP / foreground managed calling without Firebase.
# Export all four before Gradle to include managed call and message notifications.
# These values identify a Firebase Android app; they are public client settings,
# NOT service-account credentials. Do not include the server private key in an APK.
# In Firebase project settings, register the exact package for the build:
#   release: com.hybes.sipper.android
#   debug:   com.hybes.sipper.android.development
# Each package has its own Firebase app ID. Use a separate test project for debug
# builds. Manual FirebaseOptions initialisation means no google-services.json or
# Google Services Gradle plugin is required; this file is not read automatically.

# mobilesdk_app_id from the Firebase Android app, shaped 1:<number>:android:<hex>.
SIPPER_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# The Firebase project's Android client API key. Restrict it to the package and
# signing certificate as appropriate for Firebase Installations/FCM. Do not reuse
# a server API key or a service-account private key here.
SIPPER_FIREBASE_API_KEY=

# Firebase/Google Cloud project ID (not its display name or numeric project number).
# Must match project_id in the portal's SIPPER_FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT JSON.
SIPPER_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# Numeric project number / GCM sender ID from the SAME Firebase project.
SIPPER_FIREBASE_SENDER_ID=

# Server setup lives only in portal/.env.example: SIPPER_FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT
# (JSON containing project_id, client_email, private_key) and SIPPER_ANDROID_PACKAGE.
# Enable FCM HTTP v1 in that project and grant the server identity permission to
# send messages. Set SIPPER_ANDROID_PACKAGE to the exact package receiving them.
# Run the portal push worker and the SIP gateway; push acceptance alone is not
# evidence of a ringing phone. Google Play services must be available on devices.
# The user must connect an eligible gateway account and enable microphone/call
# notifications in Accounts. Direct SIP accounts do not register an FCM token.
# Call notifications use the app's phoneCall foreground service (manifest permissions
# FOREGROUND_SERVICE and FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL); it needs no extra key.
# Incoming calls use a ringtone channel and USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT to open the
# dedicated call screen. Android 14+ can deny full-screen access; Accounts exposes
# the system settings link when disabled. Ongoing calls use a silent channel.
# The user controls these permissions/channels; no environment key enables them.
# The Android 16 emulator verifies actual Telecom sessions and notification cleanup,
# plus ringtone/silent-channel and full-screen intent fields, not actual
# notification-button/lock-screen interaction or delivery through Firebase.
# API 26–30 uses WorkManager's temporary foreground notification during wake;
# newer Android uses expedited work when the received push has high priority.
# Normal-priority/deferred work still checks the call's expiry before doing work.
# No SIP password, caller name, caller number or token is stored in work input.

# Managed meetings reuse the configured portal URL and enrolled-device sign-in.
# Apply portal migration 036. There is NO additional Android meeting key:
# the authenticated portal supplies short-lived room/session grants in memory.
# Never embed SIPPER_MEETING_API_SECRET or SIPPER_MEETING_ADMISSION_KEY in an APK.
# All five meeting settings live only in portal/.env.example; proxy/media examples
# are gateway/meeting.env.example, infra/livekit.yaml.example and infra/.env.example.
# Production grants must point at the public WSS Sipper signalling proxy, not the
# private LiveKit origin. Local WS is restricted to loopback with an explicitly
# enabled local development client; an emulator host alias is not loopback.
# Join starts with microphone/camera off. Capture buttons use Android's normal
# permission flow; no environment setting enables or grants capture access.
# Metadata expires within 30 seconds independently of delayed polls. Known access
# loss stops media; grants are not archived. Phone and meeting audio are exclusive.
# The current Android meeting screen stops media on activity STOP/background and
# requires explicit rejoin. A meeting Telecom/foreground-service owner, screen
# publishing, device choices and seamless phone handoff remain implementation work.
# Real SFU/TURN/TLS media, physical capture and lock-screen acceptance are pending.
